15:# Design analysis for the D15 variant
16:# ----------------------------------
17:# spec_variant:
18:#   core idea:
19:#     - Keep the original object vocabulary: green background, blue river, black mover square,
20:#       red destination square, same-size solid squares, mover origin becomes river in output.
21:#     - Replace "the river geometry itself tells you where to turn" with a symbolic rule:
22:#       a grey legend strip on the top edge says whether each obstacle color means
23:#       clockwise or counterclockwise turning.
24:#   concrete pixel structure:
25:#     - The top 2 rows are a dedicated legend region with grey background (5).
26:#     - Each legend entry uses exactly two same-colored pixels:
27:#         row 0: one anchor pixel
28:#         row 1: one pixel shifted left  -> this color means CCW
29:#         row 1: one pixel shifted right -> this color means CW
30:#     - The playfield below still contains a blue corridor and the black/red n x n squares.
31:#     - At each turning site the corridor becomes a T-junction:
32:#         straight ahead is blocked by a 1-pixel-thick colored strip,
33:#         left and right branches are both visible,
34:#         the legend decides which branch the mover takes.
35:#   behavior trace:
36:#     - Original:
37:#         a winding blue path already contains the bends, so BFS on feasible n x n placements
38:#         can recover the final position without reading any extra symbol.
39:#     - Variant:
40:#         the blue river alone is ambiguous at turns because both side branches are present.
41:#         When the mover reaches a dead-end wall, it reads the wall's color, consults the top
42:#         legend, rotates its direction CW/CCW, and continues along that chosen branch.
43:#         If it later meets a narrowed gate (< n wide), it stops at the farthest feasible
44:#         placement before that gate, as in the original task.
45:#     - Difference:
46:#         path geometry no longer fully determines motion; the legend mediates every bend.
47:#   complexity calibration:
48:#     - Use exactly two obstacle colors per sample, one mapped to CW and one to CCW.
49:#       Both colors are reused across multiple turns, so the mapping has repeated evidence.
50:#     - Every generated sample has 3-5 turn events, so the new behavior is visually salient.
51:#     - Each turn shows both side branches, preventing degeneration back to a single obvious path.
52:#   implicit assumptions review for main():
53:#     - The original code assumes the largest non-bg non-square color is the river.
54:#       That still works after cropping away the legend because obstacle strips are thin and
55:#       river area dominates; generator enforces that.
56:#     - The original BFS assumes all choices are encoded by pure geometry.
57:#       That no longer holds, so main() must simulate directional motion with turn-state.
58:#     - New assumption: the legend occupies the top 2 rows and grey (5) is reserved for it.
59:#       Generator guarantees grey never appears in the playfield.
60:#     - New assumption: the mover's start cell has a unique outgoing feasible direction.
61:#       Generator constructs the start as a corridor endpoint.
62:#     - New assumption: when forward motion is blocked at a turn site, the blocking face has
63:#       exactly one non-river control color. Generator uses one colored strip per turn wall.
64:#   placement legality:
65:#     - Coarse nodes are laid out on a regular lattice with gaps >= n+2, so separate branches
66:#       do not accidentally merge when rendered as n-wide corridors.
67:#     - Decoy branches are only one coarse step long and are reserved during route construction
68:#       to avoid overlaps with the main route.
69:#     - Chokepoints are inserted only on a straight tail segment with enough pixel length.
70:#   visual observability:
71:#     - The legend is a dedicated top-edge strip and remains unchanged in the output.
72:#     - The same colors appear in the legend and on the turning walls, giving direct support
73:#       for the symbolic mapping from color to turn direction.
74:#     - Because both branches at each turn are visibly open, a solver must explain why the mover
75:#       chose one branch rather than the other; that explanation is exactly the legend.
76:#   cleanliness:
77:#     - The legend is isolated from the playfield.
78:#     - Obstacles are thin strips at corridor ends rather than overlapping blobs.
79:#     - Only two extra colors are introduced, keeping the scene readable.
80:#   color inference:
81:#     - No ordering depends on numeric color ids. The legend explicitly states the mapping.
82:#     - The generator shuffles which obstacle color is CW vs CCW across samples, so the solver
83:#       cannot rely on fixed color semantics.
